%I A400277 #22 Oct 09 2026 16:05:43
%S A400277 1,1,0,4,4,4,4,7,6,5,4,3,4,6,0,13,4,17,4,1,12,8,4,14,10,9,16,28,4,2,4,
%T A400277 25,26,11,0,33,4,12,16,10,4,15,4,40,24,14,4,11,14,43,36,46,4,49,32,6,
%U A400277 20,17,4,12,4,18,16,49,60,65,4,58,46,8,4,65,4,21,4,64,12,23,4,52
%N A400277 a(n) = phi(n)^2 mod (n+1), where phi is Euler's totient function (A000010).
%C A400277 a(n) = 0 iff n+1 divides phi(n)^2.
%C A400277 a(n) = n iff n+1 divides phi(n)^2 + 1.
%C A400277 The only prime power n with a(n) = n is n = 4 = 2^2.
%C A400277 The only prime power n with a(n) = 0 is n = 3.
%C A400277 Conjecture: no algebraic family exists for the composite n with a(n) = 4 (checked up to 10^8: 4, 6, 75, 132, 195, 272, 1275, 1332, 4246, 5187, 10947, 14755, 23493, 40795, 73776, 88101, 327675, 4144209, 5883744, 7478470, 33412795, 92644745).
%C A400277 Empirically, the mean value of a(n)/(n+1) over 1 <= n <= 10^8 is approximately 0.478, suggesting near-equidistribution of a(n) in [0, n]; no proof of this is known.
%F A400277 a(n) = A127473(n) mod (n+1).
%F A400277 a(p^k) = 2*p^(k-1) - p^(k-2) + 1 for prime p, k >= 2.
%F A400277 a(p^(k+1)) = p*a(p^k) - (p-1) for prime p, k >= 2.
%F A400277 a(p) = 4 for prime p >= 5.
%F A400277 For p prime, a(p-1) = A046144(p)^2 mod p.
%e A400277 a(3) = 0: phi(3) = 2, 2^2 = 4 = 1*(3+1) + 0.
%e A400277 a(9) = 6: p=3, k=2 in the prime-power formula gives 2*3 - 1 + 1 = 6.
%e A400277 a(16) = 13: p=2, k=4 gives 2*2^3 - 2^2 + 1 = 16 - 4 + 1 = 13. Recurrence check: a(16) = 2*a(8) - 1 = 2*7 - 1 = 13.
%e A400277 a(20) = 1: phi(20) = 8, 8^2 = 64 = 3*21 + 1.
%e A400277 a(4) = 4: three independent checks agree at n=4 -- the prime-power formula (2*2=4), the primitive-root formula (A046144(5) = phi(4) = 2, so 2^2 mod 5 = 4), and it is the unique prime power that is a fixed point.
%o A400277 (PARI) a(n) = eulerphi(n)^2 % (n+1);
%Y A400277 Cf. A000010, A046144.
%K A400277 nonn,easy,new
%O A400277 1,4
